   "chapeau": "Grand Theft Auto VI officially releases on November 19, but the exact time you can play depends on your location. Here's how you can check it out before everyone else.",
   "txt": "Grand Theft Auto 6 is nearly here, and we can hardly wait. Between the high-profile leaks and record-breaking extended release trailer , there's no doubt this game will be one of the biggest titles of the year.\nAfter years of anticipation and multiple delays, GTA 6 will finally be released on November 19, 2026, though the exact release time varies by time zone. If you just can't wait until it unlocks in your region, there's a trick to access the game sooner, depending on which console you're playing on.\nHere's when Grand Theft Auto 6 will unlock on your console, and how to potentially get it nearly one day sooner.\nWhen GTA 6 releases, it likely won't be unlocked for everyone simultaneously. Instead, the game is expected to be playable by region. Investigating Sony's regional store timestamps, fansite GTAVice reported that the game will have 15 release windows across 19 hours.\nThe release will begin in New Zealand, where the game will launch on November 18 at 11:00 a.m. UTC (12 a.m. NZDT), before unlocking in Australia, then across Asia and the Middle East. By 22:00 UTC, the game will become available in Eastern European countries, before reaching the rest of Europe an hour later. GTA 6 will unlock in the UK by November 19 at 00:00 UTC (12:00 a.m. GMT). South American countries will gain access before North America.\nIt's like that the United States will receive GTA 6 at the same time (midnight ET), while Canada will have a slightly staggered release:\nSt. John's, Newfoundland and Labrador: 1:30 a.m. NST (November 19)\nThese release times have not been confirmed by Sony or Rockstar, and are subject to change. Rockstar has released official preload times that start on November 10th.\nDon't want to wait for your copy of the game to unlock? You may be able to get early access by updating your gaming console's time zone.\nSince New Zealand gets the game first, Xbox owners can change their console's region to New Zealand to try to access the game once it hits 12 a.m. NZDT (8 a.m. ET).\nTo change your Xbox region settings to New Zealand, follow these steps:\nIf you're playing on PlayStation 5 , this process may be possible, but it's a bit more complicated since your account is region-locked. To make this work, you'd have to create a separate PSN account, likely using a virtual private network (VPN) that is set to New Zealand. You'd then have to purchase a copy of the game through the New Zealand PlayStation Store . This may require a payment method compatible with the region and might also cost you more after converting currencies. Even after all of this, there's no guarantee it will actually work.\nWe Got a GTA 6 PC Before We Got GTA 6 Is The Timezone Trick Worth the Effort? Changing your time zone could get you early access to GTA 6, as it's worked for other games like Call of Duty. But it's not a guarantee. \"It’s likely that Rockstar would find a way to stop this,\" said Jordan Minor , PCMag's gaming expert.\nIf you want to change your time zone on your Xbox, it's a minimal-effort step that may let you play the game roughly 18 to 19 hours earlier. The PlayStation workaround, however, is much trickier and may not be worth your time.\nHaven't preordered your copy of GTA 6 yet? Here's how to make sure you're ready to go when the game drops in your time zone.\nExperience As PCMag's editor of how to content, I have to cover a wide variety of topics and also make our stories accessible to everyday users. Considering my history as a technical writer, copywriter, and all-around freelancer covering baseball, comics, and more at various outlets, I am used to making myself into an expert.\nI believe tech corporations are bad, but you might as well know how to use technology in everyday life. Want more how to content delivered right to your inbox? Sign up for the tips and tricks newsletter that I curate three times a week.\nMy job as how-to guru means I use just about every gadget under the sun, so I can figure out how everything works. I work from a Lenovo ThinkPad running Windows 11, but also have a very large Dell Inspiron 17 3000 and Apple silicon MacBook. I have a Google Pixel 6a for personal use and use a Galaxy Z Flip 4 for additional Samsung-related testing. For iOS coverage, an iPhone 13 mini works like a charm, though it's already becoming a little long in the tooth.\nMy desktop situation includes a dual monitor setup with a modest Acer monitor. I also use a Logitech vertical mouse (who can use these ThinkPad trackpads) and a Havit keybo",
