   "txt": "GTA 6 Could Take Up 210GB on PS5, Nearly 50,000 MP3 Songs Could Fit in the Same Space Om Singh | November 3 | Industry & Events , Spotlight Share\nRockstar Games’ upcoming title GTA 6 could demand a substantial amount of storage space on gaming consoles, with a new leak suggesting that the game may require approximately 210GB for installation. If the information proves accurate, the highly anticipated title could occupy a significant portion of the storage available on a standard PlayStation 5.\nThe reported download size also offers an interesting comparison with the gaming and digital entertainment landscape of 2010. Back then, 200GB was enough to accommodate dozens of popular PC games or even tens of thousands of MP3 music tracks. Today, a single upcoming game could potentially require almost the same amount of space.\nGTA 6 is scheduled to release on November 19, 2026, following a wait of 13 years since the previous mainline instalment in the franchise. With players preparing to return to Vice City, storage requirements could become another consideration before the game’s launch.\nThe reported 210GB download size originated from a leak shared by X user @PlayStation Size . Gaming publication Insider Gaming has also reported on the claim.\nHowever, Rockstar Games has not officially confirmed the game’s final download size. The figure should therefore be treated as an unverified estimate rather than an established requirement.\nIf the reported size turns out to be accurate, GTA 6 could consume roughly one-fourth of the PlayStation 5’s advertised 825GB storage capacity. That could leave considerably less room for players who maintain a large collection of installed games.\nThe actual amount of usable storage on a console also differs from its advertised capacity, making the available space an important consideration.\nThe reported storage requirement becomes particularly interesting when compared with the size of popular games from earlier years.\nIn 2010, a 200GB drive could accommodate multiple copies of games that were widely played during that period. The original PC version of GTA Vice City required approximately 1.55GB of hard drive space, while GTA San Andreas needed around 4.7GB.\nOther popular titles were similarly smaller in size. Need for Speed: Most Wanted (2005) required approximately 3GB, while GTA III could run with around 500MB of storage.\nThese comparisons highlight how dramatically game storage requirements have changed over the years.\nGaming was not the only area where 200GB offered considerably more storage capacity in 2010. Digital music collections were also much easier to accommodate.\nAn average MP3 song could take approximately 4MB of storage space. Based on that estimate, a 200GB drive could theoretically hold close to 50,000 songs.\nThis makes the reported GTA 6 requirement particularly striking. The storage space that could once accommodate an enormous music collection or dozens of older games might now be needed for a single modern gaming title.\nWhile the 210GB figure remains unconfirmed, the leak has brought attention to an increasingly important aspect of modern gaming: the amount of storage required to install and maintain major releases.\nAlso Read: The Man Who Turned India’s Sportswear Shortage Into a ₹235 Crore Business\nOm Singh is an AI enthusiast, helping budding founders leverage technology to grow their businesses.",
