   "t": "GTA 6 Cyberleek Is Back as Coin Drops to $1.2M",
   "u": "https://shattered.io/gta-6-cyberleek-ends-leaks-coin-1-2-million-2026/",
   "img": "https://shattered.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/gta-6-cyberleek-ends-leaks-coin-1-2-million-2026-gen.jpg",
   "chapeau": "GTA 6 leaker Cyberleek posted a final video, then said it was back after the $CYBERLEEK token crashed from $7.8M to $1.2M.",
   "txt": "Grand Theft Auto VI’s most persistent leaker just tried to close the book on a saga that has run for months. On October 14, 2026, the anonymous figure known as Cyberleek posted a new video, which IGN reported as a 24-minute gameplay video, carrying the title “The End of the Road.” Mashable reported that Cyberleek shared roughly 20 clips, which reportedly include Lucia at a barbershop, a shop robbery, a pool scene, several locations, and flyover footage. Cyberleek framed the release as the final chapter of a leak campaign that had, by its own telling, turned into a cryptocurrency operation. Rockstar Games has not confirmed any of the footage, and the claim that these clips come from the game’s final missions remains unverified.\nWhat makes October 14 different from Cyberleek’s earlier posts is the money trail attached to it. A token called $CYBERLEEK, built on a blockchain and tied directly to the leak campaign, hit a reported peak market capitalization of $7.8 million on October 11, 2026. Within three days, that figure had fallen to roughly $1.2 million, according to IGN, or about $1.7 million per a separate figure reported by Enduins, and later reporting still places the coin near $1.2 million in market capitalization on November 2, 2026. Whichever number you use, the token lost well over three-quarters of its value in under 72 hours. Cyberleek’s own framing was blunt: the leaks stop here, for now, and the ending stays unpublished.\nGoogle · Preferred Sources Don't miss new tech stories on Google\nAdd Shattered once in the Google app and our stories show up in your news suggestions.\nThe new video runs approximately four minutes, a modest length compared to some of Cyberleek’s earlier drops. According to reporting on the release, the footage shows short clips centered on Lucia, one of GTA 6’s two confirmed protagonists, moving through a barbershop, taking part in a shop robbery, lounging by a pool, and passing through assorted map locations, capped by flyover footage of the game world. Cyberleek described these as pulled from the game’s final missions. That claim has not been independently verified, and separate reporting notes the footage does not clearly establish a connection to a final mission or show anything resembling an actual ending cutscene.\nThat distinction matters more than it might seem. Dexerto’s coverage of the release framed it as Cyberleek posting “parts of final missions” while stopping the broader leak campaign, a characterization that leaves plenty of room between what was shown and what was promised. Eurogamer’s report on the same video used similarly careful language, describing the clips as allegedly showing scenes from the final mission while noting the leaks may have come to an end rather than stating outright that they have. The gap between “allegedly” and “confirmed” is the entire story here, and it’s one Rockstar has done nothing to close.\nCyberleek’s message accompanying the video leaned on two phrases that have since been repeated across gaming coverage: “The leeks stop here. For now.” and “The ending stays unpublished.” Read together, those lines do two things at once. They announce a halt to new leaks while deliberately leaving the door open with “for now,” and they draw a hard line around the one piece of content Cyberleek says it will withhold regardless of what happens next, the actual ending.\nThat second phrase is worth sitting with. If the campaign really does have footage beyond what’s been shown, holding back the ending is the one piece of leverage that survives a collapsed token. Whether that leverage is real or just the last card in a bluff is exactly what nobody outside Cyberleek’s circle can confirm. Enduins, one of the outlets tracking the saga closely, titled its own report on the episode “Cyberleek Ends GTA VI Leaks After Memecoin Collapses,” tying the stoppage directly to the token’s crash rather than treating it as a planned conclusion.\nThe numbers behind $CYBERLEEK tell a familiar story to anyone who has watched a thin-liquidity memecoin move. The token reportedly climbed to a peak market capitalization of $7.8 million on October 11, 2026, the same week Cyberleek was promising bigger disclosures tied to higher valuations. By October 13 and 14, that figure had cratered. IGN put the post-collapse market cap at roughly $1.2 million. Enduins reported a slightly higher figure, around $1.7 million. Separately, Yahoo Finance, citing CoinGecko data, reported a steep single-day de",
